ZTEXT001000030dc8454ec6d0000000
Ezero language guide

Ezero source is UTF-8. The frontend validates the complete input, including
comments and optional quoted text, and rejects malformed UTF-8 and embedded
NUL. A newline ends an ordinary statement. Blank lines are allowed; # starts
a comment through the end of the line. Indentation is for readability.
Square brackets delimit blocks and lists; | separates list items and arguments.
There are no required type annotations, @ prefixes or statement-ending dots.

Values and bindings
    10 => count
    count + 5 => count
    count => show

=> creates a binding on first assignment and updates it on later assignments.
Types follow the stored values automatically; a binding can later hold another
value kind. Ezero exposes four runtime value kinds:
- Number: an unsigned integer from 0 through 18446744073709551615.
- Text: immutable UTF-8 bytes.
- List: an ordered immutable collection, including mixed or nested values.
- Phrase: ordered typed parts displayed with spaces instead of list brackets.

true and false are numeric 1 and 0. empty is empty text. No pointer arithmetic,
manual allocation or manual free operation is exposed.

Text and typed phrases
    Mira => name
    Hello name => show
    3 yellow bananas => purchase
    purchase[1] + 2 => show
    purchase[2] => show

An unbound bare word is literal text. An already-known binding name reads its
value. Consecutive unbound literal words become one text segment with a single
space between words. Adjacent values form a phrase without turning numbers
into text. purchase above has two parts: number 3 and text yellow bananas.
The last two lines print 5 and yellow bananas.

Names used for bindings, parameters and functions are case-sensitive ASCII:
1..31 characters, starting with a letter or underscore, then letters, digits
or underscores. Other UTF-8 words are text. Unknown bare words intentionally
become text, so a misspelled binding name can become literal text too.
A binding introduced by a later statement is not known at an earlier use.
A known binding whose assignment did not run produces an uninitialized-value
error when read; for example a binding introduced in a false when branch.

Phrases preserve separate values, including separate bound text values. The
compiler only merges consecutive literal words. Thus literal Hello Mira is
one text value, while Hello name is a phrase containing text Hello and the
value of name. Both can print Hello Mira, but size and indexing differ.
Text can be written without quotes in ordinary examples. Optional double
quotes provide literal text when an exact binding name, reserved word,
operator character, repeated whitespace or escaped newline is needed.
Supported escapes are \n, \r, \t, \\ and \". A quoted numeric spelling remains
text; an ordinary numeric token remains a number, including inside a phrase.

Reserved words
show return repeat when each size true false empty
readfile writefile fileexists arguments host lines join
read save create del rm watch search go disks
These words cannot be binding/function/parameter names. Operator punctuation
such as + - * => == brackets and parentheses also has syntax meaning. Bare
punctuation outside those operators, for example Hello!, can remain text.
A leading ASCII drive letter followed by :| is kept inside a bare path word:
A:|note and B:| are text without required quotes. Elsewhere | still separates
arguments and list items. Wildcards * and ? inside paths use optional quotes
or text bindings because * is also an arithmetic operator.

Lists, phrases and one-based indexing
    [10 | 20 | 30] => numbers
    numbers[1] => show
    size(numbers) => show
    numbers => each number [
        number * 2 => show
    ]

The first two outputs are 10 and 3. each then prints 20, 40, 60. It evaluates the
collection once and visits elements in order. It accepts lists and phrases.
Indices must be numbers from 1 through size(collection). Zero, an out-of-range
index and a nonnumeric index are errors. Text indexing is not implemented.
size(text) counts Unicode scalar values, not UTF-8 bytes or grapheme clusters.
For example size(банана) is 6. size(list) and size(phrase) count elements.
Nested lists are valid, for example [[1 | 2] | [3 | 4]]. Each selected inner
list can be indexed again. Empty lists are written []. Collections are
immutable; assigning a new value changes a binding, not an existing object.

Lines and joining text
    [First | Second | Third] => names
    join(names | /) => show
    lines("First\nSecond\r\nThird")[2] => show

The outputs are First/Second/Third and Second. join(list | separator) joins
an ordered list of text values into one text value. It inserts the separator
only between items. The separator must be text even for an empty list; every
item must also be text. Numbers, phrases and nested lists are explicit errors,
not implicit string conversions. join([] | /) returns empty text; an empty
separator concatenates the items without extra bytes.

lines(text) splits at LF or CRLF and returns a one-based list of text values.
Empty input returns []; blank lines and the empty final line after a trailing
newline are retained. A standalone CR remains ordinary text. A CR immediately
before LF is removed with that separator; no other whitespace is trimmed.
Unicode bytes are preserved. At most 2048 resulting lines are allowed; more
report a source-line error. join has no 2048-item input limit: it accepts any
managed text list whose complete result fits the existing managed heap.
Both operations allocate immutable results and work in Zero OS as well as on
Linux and Windows. They do not access the filesystem. To process a host file,
compose lines(readfile(path)); creating a new text file can use
writefile(newpath | join(items | "\n")). Existing files remain protected by
writefile's exclusive-create contract.

Arithmetic and comparisons
Precedence, highest first: calls/indexing/grouping, multiplication, addition
and subtraction, one comparison, adjacent phrase parts. Parentheses group an
expression. Function arguments, list items and grouped expressions can span
lines. Chained comparisons require explicit grouping.

+ adds numbers with overflow checking; joins text without adding a space;
joins text and a number after decimal formatting; or concatenates two lists.
Thus 3 bananas preserves a numeric phrase part, while 3+bananas produces text
3bananas. Phrase concatenation with + is not supported.
- and * require numbers. Negative subtraction and multiplication overflow
are reported errors. There is no unary minus, division, floating point or
implicit arithmetic wraparound.

== and != compare numbers or text by content. A number and text are different
value kinds and compare unequal. Text equality uses its UTF-8 bytes without
normalization. List and phrase equality are rejected in this version.
< <= > >= require numbers. Comparisons produce numeric 0 or 1.

Output and controls
value => show prints a number in decimal, text directly, a list with [ ] and
' | ' separators, or a phrase with spaces. It adds a newline.

    3 => repeat [
        Hello => show
    ]

repeat evaluates its count once. The count must be a number; zero performs no
iterations. Its internal counter is separate from user bindings.

    count > 10 => when [
        More than ten => show
    ] ~ [
        Ten or less => show
    ]

when requires a number: zero chooses the optional ~ branch, any nonzero value
chooses the first branch. A newline can precede ~. Blocks may contain other
controls. Each ordinary statement ends at a newline or the block's closing ].

Functions
    double :: value [
        value * 2 => return
    ]
    double(21) => show

    describe :: amount | item [
        amount item => return
    ]
    describe(3 | bananas) => show

Functions are defined at top level before use. A function can call itself;
recursion is supported. Definitions can have zero parameters. Calls pass
arguments left to right with | separators and require the exact parameter
count. return supplies one result. Reaching the end returns numeric zero.
return outside a function is a compile error.

Parameters and assignments inside a function are local to that call. Reads
first resolve known local bindings, then globals declared before the function
was compiled. A local assignment can shadow a global; it does not update that
global. Callers retain their local values and pending expression values during
nested calls and collection. There are no closures, nested definitions,
imports, first-class function values or automatic forward declarations.

Automatic memory and limits
Text, lists and phrases use a bounded copying garbage collector. Live values
in globals, active function frames and the evaluation stack remain reachable.
Dead heap objects are reclaimed automatically. Each heap semispace is 262144
bytes (256 KiB); live objects plus the next allocation must fit one semispace.
Object headers and alignment consume part of that capacity. Exhaustion is an
explicit error, not unlimited growth or a raw invalid memory access.

Current fixed capacities:
- Source: 65536 bytes; decoded text segment: 4096 bytes.
- All text constants: 16384 bytes; generated instructions: 1536.
- Functions: 64 including the main program; parameters: 16 per function.
- Bindings: 256 per scope, including hidden loop slots.
- Expression and block nesting: 32 levels.
- Evaluation values: 2048; active function calls: 64.
Further details, including exact runtime layout, are in MEMORY.ztext and ABI.ztext.

Diagnostics and execution boundary
Compilation errors report the Ezero source line. The native compiler command
reports these diagnostics and creates output only after compilation succeeds.
Runtime errors report a source line and exit 65; earlier output can remain. Runtime diagnostic
output currently uses stdout through Zero's existing host-I/O interface.
Output I/O failures retain the existing Zero exit 74 behavior.

The frontend, packer and interpreter/runtime are written in original Zero.
The frontend produces bounded instructions; the packer emits their Zero
initialization. That initialization and runtime are compiled together by our
existing Zero compiler into a standalone Linux ELF, Windows PE or Zero OS
native application. Instructions
are interpreted by the embedded original runtime; direct native emission for
each Ezero expression is not implemented. No external runtime library is
required by the generated executable. Native compiler commands and the
original Zero build/check tools perform the pipeline in-process. Zero OS includes its own guest loader and
compiler bundle; its terminal accepts bounded inline source.

Files and volumes
read(path) and readfile(path) return strict UTF-8 text. save(path | text)
creates or replaces content; writefile(path | text) exclusively creates a new
file and preserves an existing destination. Both return written byte counts.
create(path) creates an empty file or preserves an existing regular file.
del(path), also spelled rm(path), deletes an existing regular file.
watch(root) and search(patternpath) return one-based lists of regular filenames.
search accepts * and ? in its last component; no wildcard means exact match.
go(root) changes the selected directory/volume; disks() returns available
volume roots. create, del and go return 1 on success; errors include the source
line. fileexists(path) is 1 for a readable regular file and 0 otherwise.

  create(notes) => ready
  save(notes | Three bananas) => bytes
  read(notes) => show
  search("note*") => each name [ name => show ]

These operations call native platform adapters or the Zero OS kernel service.
These are raw-content operations; they do not implicitly encode or decode
ZTEXT001. The separate original zero-text command manages that checked format.
Native files allow up to 65536 UTF-8 bytes; the current writable ZeroFS guest
profile has flat names up to 31 ASCII bytes and files up to 400 printable ASCII
bytes. Unavailable operations and unsupported content report errors. save is
explicit replacement, not an atomic transaction. See HOST.ztext for guarantees,
listing limits, glob behavior and precise platform differences.

arguments() returns native user arguments as a one-based list; it is not
available in Zero OS. host() returns 1 for Linux, 2 for Windows, 0 for Zero OS.
File APIs need no blanket native-host guard. Builtin names are reserved words;
use optional literal quotes when one must occur as plain text.
